          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does the thyroid do, and what goes wrong in hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "The thyroid is a small gland in the neck. It makes mainly thyroxine, which the body converts into a more active hormone, triiodothyronine. Together these set the pace of metabolism in most of the body. The pituitary gland, in the brain, watches the level and releases thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) to prompt the thyroid to work harder or less hard. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nHypothyroidism means the thyroid does not make enough of this hormone. In most cases the gland itself is at fault, which is called primary hypothyroidism. Rarely, the problem lies with the pituitary or hypothalamus, which is called central hypothyroidism. (Same source.) The guidance I found names one cause: autoimmune thyroiditis, where the immune system attacks the thyroid. It is identified by testing for thyroid peroxidase antibodies. (Source: *Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*.) The passages I retrieved did not list other causes, so I cannot give a full list.\n\nTesting usually starts with TSH. A high TSH with a low free thyroxine suggests overt hypothyroidism. A high TSH with a normal free thyroxine suggests subclinical hypothyroidism. (Same guideline.)\n\nI could not find lifestyle guidance specific to hypothyroidism. The lifestyle results I retrieved covered blood pressure, type 2 diabetes and heart failure, so I have not applied them here. A clinician can advise on what, if anything, applies to your situation.\n\nIf you have symptoms you are worried about, or you have been told you have an underactive thyroid and are unsure what it means for you, a doctor or pharmacist is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
